findings.notes:
H is stated as falsifiable per-slice inequalities on unrounded deltas: delta ≥ −1.0 on each of english-general, tool-call, long-context and refusal, AND delta_non-english < −1.0.

The non-English regression is correctly treated as a prediction, not a refutation. The rule is one-sided on the named slices. The boundary cases are handled explicitly, and I checked the item-count bounds (98/135/1/232 and ≤72) against the baseline counts in results.json (art_40be1e74).

The listed alternatives are each distinguishable:
- (a) a uniform drop on all slices;
- (b) a hidden single-slice regression (no aggregate is used);
- (c) non-English holding up;
- (d) nondeterminism or machine drift, via the replay control.

Alternative (e), judge self-preference, is named but cannot be controlled under the frozen protocol. The report must flag that it is confounded with the intervention.

Memory fits:
- 3B bf16 weights are about 5.75 GiB. KV at 36,864 B/token (arithmetic verified) gives about 400k tokens of capacity, against the about 99k tokens the frozen 7B completed the same workload with.
- The longest request is 13,426+512 tokens, below max_model_len 20000.

Time and money are realistic: about 2.3 h (4 h with contingency) and about $5, with a hard stop at $15, under the $60 cap. The order of work puts the configuration check before any timed run.

Stop conditions are declared: OOM, a failed configuration check, and running out of funds or disk. The run must never reduce concurrency or truncate contexts. Fallback (2), no GPU means no run and no simulation, is honoured.

The limitations are stated clearly:
- the 3B/7B stand-in does not settle the 32B/70B C2;
- multilingual fall-off may not scale between sizes;
- 1-GPU throughput does not transfer to the 70B route;
- judge bias;
- resolution;
- the tool-call floor.

The unmeasured judge-session drift should additionally be noted in the report.
